<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sumatra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sumatra is a large island in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, lying along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a>. It was the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> controlled the island in turn.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumatra.jpg" alt="Sumatra" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sadalmelik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sumatra_Topography.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aceh grew up at the northern tip of <b>Sumatra</b>, where the Strait of Malacca opens onto the Indian Ocean, an area earlier dominated by the port-kingdom of Lamuri and by Samudera Pasai, one of the first Muslim states of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cham language belongs to the Austronesian family and is most closely related to Acehnese in northern <b>Sumatra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tobacco estates in Deli on <b>Sumatra</b>, sugar mills on Java, tin on Bangka and Belitung, rubber and, from the 1890s, oil drilled by the Royal Dutch company made the Indies one of the world’s most valuable colonies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 settled the division of the region: the Dutch ceded Malacca and their Indian posts and accepted British possession of Singapore, while Britain gave up Bencoolen and its claims in <b>Sumatra</b>, leaving the archipelago south of the Strait of Malacca to the Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The shift of trade to <b>Sumatra</b>, the rise of Srivijaya and China's taking of the routes through Southeast Asia brought economic instability to the south and forced politics and the economy northward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Indonesia spans some 17,000 islands, from <b>Sumatra</b> to New Guinea, and its population has grown from about 70 million in 1950 to around 280 million, more than half of them on Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
